rs` and in the dead-candidate collection). This is both a **duplication** and a potential **clarity** issue: both inner loops use the same 3×3 neighbor pattern.\n\n### Rule 3: No Duplication\nThe nested neighbor-offset loop (`for dx = -1..1, for dy = -1..1, skip (0,0)`) appears **twice**:\n1. Inside `countNeighbors` — to count live neighbors\n2. Inside the main loop — to collect dead candidates\n\nThis is clear duplication — the same 8-neighbor iteration pattern is duplicated.\n\n### Rule 4: Fewest Elements\nI notice the `deadCandidates` approach has two phases (collect, then process) which involves two passes over data — potentially more elements than needed.\n\n---\n\n## Step 4: Implement Refactoring\n\nThe most impactful refactoring: **Extract the neighbor-offset loop into a shared helper**, or better yet, **use a single-pass approach** that computes both survival and reproduction in one neighbor-scan.\n\nActually, let me think about a cleaner approach. Instead of two nested offset loops, I can compute counts for all cells (live and neighbor dead cells) in a single pass using a Map. This eliminates both the duplicated nested loops AND the two-phase dead-candidate pattern.\n\nLet me implement this:\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_ca8e5e9777ba4eeea2a7deb0","name":"edit","arguments":{"path":"src/game-of-life.ts","edits":[{"oldText":"export function nextGeneration(cells: [number, number][]): [number, number][] {\n  // Build a set of live cells for O(1) lookup\n  const liveSet = new Set(cells.map(([x, y]) => `${x},${y}`));\n\n  // Count neighbors for a given cell\n  const countNeighbors = (x: number, y: number): number => {\n    let count = 0;\n    for (let dx = -1; dx <= 1; dx++) {\n      for (let dy = -1; dy <= 1; dy++) {\n        if (dx === 0 && dy === 0) continue;\n        if (liveSet.has(`${x + dx},${y + dy}`)) {\n          count++;\n        }\n      }\n    }\n    return count;\n  };\n\n  const result: [number, number][] = [];\n  const deadCandidates = new Set<string>();\n\n  for (const [x, y] of cells) {\n    const neighbors = countNeighbors(x, y);\n    // Survival: live cell with 2 or 3 neighbors lives on\n    if (neighbors === 2 || neighbors === 3) {\n      result.push([x, y]);\n    }\n\n    // Collect dead neighbor positions as reproduction candidates\n    for (let dx = -1; dx <= 1; dx++) {\n      for (let dy = -1; dy <= 1; dy++) {\n        if (dx === 0 && dy === 0) continue;\n        const nx = x + dx;\n        const ny = y + dy;\n        if (!liveSet.has(`${nx},${ny}`)) {\n          deadCandidates.add(`${nx},${ny}`);\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n\n  // Check dead candidates for reproduction\n  for (const key of deadCandidates) {\n    const [nx, ny] = key.split(\",\").map(Number) as [number, number];\n    if (countNeighbors(nx, ny) === 3) {\n      result.push([nx, ny]);\n    }\n  }\n\n  return result;\n}","newText":"export function nextGeneration(cells: [number, number][]): [number, number][] {\n  const liveSet = new Set(cells.map(([x, y]) => `${x},${y}`));\n\n  // Count live neighbors for every cell and its adjacent dead cells in one pass\n  const neighborCounts = new Map<string, number>();\n  for (const [x, y] of cells) {\n    for (let dx = -1; dx <= 1; dx++) {\n      for (let dy = -1; dy <= 1; dy++) {\n        if (dx === 0 && dy === 0) continue;\n        const key = `${x + dx},${y + dy}`;\n        neighborCounts.set(key, (neighborCounts.get(key) || 0) + 1);\n      }\n    }\n  }\n\n  // Apply rules: survival (live + 2/3 neighbors) and reproduction (dead + 3)\n  const result: [number, number][] = [];\n  for (const [key, count] of neighborCounts) {\n    if (count === 3 || (count === 2 && liveSet.has(key))) {\n      const [nx, ny] = key.split(\",\").map(Number) as [number, number];\n      result.push([nx, ny]);\n    }\n  }\n\n  return result;\n}"}]}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/deepseek-v4-pro","usage":{"input":544,"output":3570,"cacheRead":8192,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0