{"type":"tool_execution_update","toolCallId":"call_04c7fe48393544b3872b7346","toolName":"subagent","args":{"agent":"refactor","agentScope":"both","task":"Test file: src/game-of-life.spec.ts\nImplementation file: src/game-of-life.ts\nPassing tests: 5\nRecent Green phase: Added reproduction logic — dead cells adjacent to live cells with exactly 3 neighbors are born.\n\nRun autonomously, return after completion."},"partialResult":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The order changed because `Map` iteration order is insertion order, and my new implementation iterates in a different order than the test expects. The test expects `[[1, 0], [1, -1], [1, 1]]` but the Map iteration gives `[[1, -1], [1, 0], [1, 1]]`. The test checks strict array equality with `toEqual`.\n\nI need to make the test assertion order-independent, or sort the result. Actually, looking at this more carefully — the data is correct, just the order differs. But the test uses `toEqual` which is order-sensitive. I should not modify the test (that's the test file, not the implementation), but I should make the implementation preserve a consistent order. Let"}],"details":{"mode":"single","agentScope":"both","projectAgentsDir":"/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_12-14-27_game-of-life-example-mapping_v6.2.1-phase-continuation-pi_deepseek-v4-pro/.pi/agents","results":[{"agent":"refactor","agentSource":"project","task":"Test file: src/game-of-life.spec.ts\nImplementation file: src/game-of-life.ts\nPassing tests: 5\nRecent Green phase: Added reproduction logic — dead cells adjacent to live cells with exactly 3 neighbors are born.\n\nRun autonomously, return after completion.","exitCode":0,"messages":[{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Task: Test file: src/game-of-life.spec.ts\nImplementation file: src/game-of-life.ts\nPassing tests: 5\nRecent Green phase: Added reproduction logic — dead cells adjacent to live cells with exactly 3 neighbors are born.\n\nRun autonomously, return after comp